<!-- ============ PROBLEM ============ -->
<section class="sec" id="problem">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Ground truth</p>
      <h2>A multiply that is not a multiply</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          A Q1_0 block holds <strong>128 weights in 16 bytes</strong>, one bit each, plus one fp16
          scale. Bit set means +1, bit clear means -1. The activations are Q8_0: 32 int8 quants
          and one fp16 scale.
        </p>
        <p>
          So the inner operation is not a multiply-accumulate. It is <strong>add or subtract the
          activation</strong>. On a machine with a 1024-bit vector register that is almost free,
          which means the kernel is decided entirely by two questions: how many lanes are doing
          useful work, and how many instructions it takes to feed them.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="panels two">
      <div class="panel">
        <div class="panel-label"><span>Weights &mdash; block_q1_0</span><span class="r">18 B</span></div>
        <div class="bytemap">
          <div class="bseg d" style="flex:2"><span>d</span><span class="n">fp16</span></div>
          <div class="bseg q striped" style="flex:16"><span>qs[16] &mdash; 128 sign bits</span><span class="n">bit c = column c</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="bytemap-key"><span>byte 0</span><span>byte 17</span></div>
        <p class="cap">One block is <b>one row, 128 columns</b>. Rows sit one after another in memory.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="panel">
        <div class="panel-label"><span>Activations &mdash; block_q8_0</span><span class="r">34 B</span></div>
        <div class="bytemap">
          <div class="bseg d" style="flex:2"><span>d</span><span class="n">fp16</span></div>
          <div class="bseg q striped" style="flex:32"><span>qs[32] &mdash; int8 quants</span><span class="n">one byte per column</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="bytemap-key"><span>byte 0</span><span>byte 33</span></div>
        <p class="cap"><b>qs starts at offset 2.</b> Remember this: it becomes a fault in stage 04.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="panels two" style="margin-top:1.8rem">
      <div class="panel">
        <div class="panel-label"><span>Inside the measured window</span><span class="r">paid every token</span></div>
        <ul class="tick">
          <li>Quantizing the activations from float to q8_0 (decode) or q8_0x4 (prefill). The
            baseline pays this too, so the comparison is like for like.</li>
          <li>Expanding the 64 fp16 row scales to fp32, once per weight block.</li>
          <li>Restaging or de-interleaving the activation quants, stage 04 onward.</li>
          <li>The gemv or gemm itself.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="panel">
        <div class="panel-label"><span>Outside it</span><span class="r">paid once per process</span></div>
        <ul class="tick out">
          <li><b>The weight repack.</b> It runs in the buffer's <code>set_tensor</code> at model
            load, single-threaded, before any graph executes. The ROI markers are not armed yet.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="cap">
          Not measured on the target, but bounded from the generated code. The repack loop is
          scalar and runs <b>once per weight bit</b>: extract the bit, compute the destination
          index, read-modify-write one byte. Compiled for rv64 at -O2 the innermost loop is
          <b>16 instructions per bit</b> in the default order and <b>21 in the Q1_D4 order</b>,
          with no unrolling.
        </p>
        <div class="tally" style="margin-top:.7rem">
          <dt>Q1_0 weights in Bonsai-4B</dt><dd>4.02 G / 566 MB</dd>
          <dt>fails the repack gate (LM head)</dt><dd>9.7%</dd>
          <dt>actually repacked</dt><dd>3.63 G</dd>
          <dt>instructions, single-threaded</dt><dd class="lo">76 G</dd>
          <dt>one decode pass, for scale</dt><dd>0.61 G cycles</dd>
        </div>
        <p class="cap">
          On an in-order core at 1 IPC that is <b>over a minute before the first token</b>, or more
          than a hundred tokens of payback. The same loop takes 4.5 s on an x86 host, where
          out-of-order execution hides the read-modify-write chain - the target cannot.
        </p>
        <p class="cap">
          It is also entirely fixable, and nobody has tried: <b>cache the repacked buffer on
          disk</b> so it becomes a build step, <b>thread it</b> (tensors and 64-row groups are
          independent; <code>set_tensor</code> is single-threaded today), or <b>vectorize the bit
          transpose</b> with the same mask operations the kernel itself uses. Any one of the three
          removes the problem.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <ul class="kv">
      <li><span class="k">Machine</span><span class="v">Andes AX46MPV, VLEN=1024. One vector register is 128 int8 lanes or 32 fp32 lanes; at LMUL=2, 64 fp32 lanes.</span></li>
      <li><span class="k">Measured node</span><span class="v">Bonsai-4B <code>blk.7.attn_v.weight</code>, K=2560, 1024 output rows. Layer 7 covers all six distinct Q1_0 shapes in the model.</span></li>
      <li><span class="k">Method</span><span class="v">andesim hybrid marker mode. The RTL engine counts cycles only between the ROI markers around one MUL_MAT node, so nothing else in the model pollutes the number.</span></li>
      <li><span class="k">Correctness</span><span class="v">13 shape cases in <code>tests/test-repack-q1_0</code>, run for both layout orders. Stages 03, 04 and 05 are bit-identical to stage 02, not merely close.</span></li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ STAGE 01 ============ -->
<section class="sec" id="s01">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="stage-head">
      <div class="stage-num">01</div>
      <div class="stage-titles">
        <p class="stage-kicker">Upstream baseline &mdash; ggml_vec_dot_q1_0_q8_0</p>
        <h2>One row at a time, and a reduction every 32 columns</h2>
        <div class="stage-cyc">
          <span class="chip">decode <b>4,476,168</b></span>
          <span class="chip">prefill <b>8,875,248</b></span>
          <span class="chip">1.00x baseline</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="panels wide-left">
      <div class="panel">
        <div class="panel-label"><span>What one step touches</span><span class="r">1 row x 32 columns</span></div>
        <div class="gridbox">
          <div class="gridwrap">
            <div class="xlab"><span>col c</span><span>c+15 of 128</span></div>
            <div class="ylab"><span>row 0</span><span>row 63</span></div>
            <div class="lanegrid dim" data-rows="64" data-cols="16" data-seed="11"
                 data-hl="row" data-hl-start="9" data-hl-span="1" data-hl-tag="32 columns, 1 output"></div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="legend">
          <span><i class="p"></i> weight +1</span>
          <span><i class="m"></i> weight -1</span>
          <span><i class="k"></i> one inner-loop step</span>
        </div>
        <p class="cap">
          The vector register holds <b>32 columns of a single row</b>. 63 of the 64 rows in this
          picture are idle while that row is computed.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="panel">
        <div class="panel-label"><span>Inner loop</span><span class="r">per 32 columns</span></div>
        <div class="ops"><span class="cm">; VLMAX at e8m1 is 128 lanes; this uses 32</span>
<span class="op">vsetvl  </span>  e8, m1, vl=32
<span class="op">vlm.v   </span>  v0, (x.qs)      <span class="cm">; 32 sign bits</span>
<span class="op">vle8.v  </span>  v8, (y.qs)      <span class="cm">; 32 activations</span>
<span class="op">vneg.v  </span>  v9, v8
<span class="op">vmerge  </span>  v10, v9, v8, v0 <span class="cm">; +-y</span>
<span class="bad">vwredsum</span>  v11, v10, zero  <span class="cm">; horizontal !</span>
<span class="op">fmadd   </span>  acc, d_y, v11[0]</div>
        <dl class="tally">
          <dt>lanes busy</dt><dd class="lo">32 of 128</dd>
          <dt>MACs per vector op</dt><dd class="lo">6.4</dd>
          <dt>vector ops per 64x128 tile</dt><dd>1,280</dd>
          <dt>horizontal reductions, this node</dt><dd class="lo">163,840</dd>
        </dl>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="pull">
      <p>
        A <code>vwredsum</code> is a serial tree. Doing 80 of them per output row is the
        single biggest thing wrong with this kernel, and no amount of tuning inside the
        loop removes it. The layout has to change.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ STAGE 02 ============ -->
<section class="sec" id="s02">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="stage-head">
      <div class="stage-num">02</div>
      <div class="stage-titles">
        <p class="stage-kicker">Repack, standard RVV &mdash; block_q1_0x64</p>
        <h2>Put 64 rows in the lanes, and the reductions disappear</h2>
        <div class="stage-cyc">
          <span class="chip win">decode <b>2,144,213</b> &nbsp;2.09x</span>
          <span class="chip win">prefill <b>1,696,704</b> &nbsp;5.23x</span>
          <span class="chip">0 horizontal reductions</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1.6rem">
      <p>
        Turn the tile 90 degrees. Lane <em>j</em> holds <strong>row j</strong>, not column j.
        The reduction over K then becomes a loop-carried vertical add inside each lane, which
        costs nothing extra, and the kernel has <strong>no horizontal reduction anywhere</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        For that to work the sign bits have to be transposed too, so that one mask load gives
        one column across all 64 rows. That is the repacked block: same 1152 bytes as 64
        original blocks, rearranged.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="panels">
      <div class="panel">
        <div class="panel-label"><span>Repacked layout &mdash; block_q1_0x64</span><span class="r">1152 B = 64 x 18 B</span></div>
        <div class="bytemap">
          <div class="bseg d" style="flex:128"><span>d[64]</span><span class="n">128 B</span></div>
          <div class="bseg q hi" style="flex:64"><span>plane 0</span><span class="n">8 B</span></div>
          <div class="bseg q" style="flex:64"><span>plane 1</span><span class="n">8 B</span></div>
          <div class="bseg q striped" style="flex:832"><span>planes 2 .. 127</span><span class="n">8 B each</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="bytemap-key"><span>byte 0</span><span>byte 1151</span></div>
        <p class="cap">
          <b>Bit index <code>c*64 + j</code>.</b> Plane <em>c</em> is 64 contiguous row bits for
          column <em>c</em>, so one 8-byte <code>vlm</code> loads a whole column as an RVV mask
          &mdash; exactly the operand the masked add wants.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="panels wide-left">
      <div class="panel">
        <div class="panel-label"><span>What one step touches</span><span class="r">64 rows x 1 column</span></div>
        <div class="gridbox">
          <div class="gridwrap">
            <div class="xlab"><span>col c</span><span>c+15 of 128</span></div>
            <div class="ylab"><span>row 0</span><span>row 63</span></div>
            <div class="lanegrid dim" data-rows="64" data-cols="16" data-seed="11"
                 data-hl="col" data-hl-start="5" data-hl-span="1" data-hl-tag="1 vlm = 64 rows"></div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="legend">
          <span><i class="p"></i> weight +1</span>
          <span><i class="m"></i> weight -1</span>
          <span><i class="k"></i> one inner-loop step</span>
        </div>
        <p class="cap">
          Every lane is now an output row. The step advances one column and all 64 accumulators
          move together.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="panel">
        <div class="panel-label"><span>Inner loop</span><span class="r">per column</span></div>
        <div class="ops"><span class="cm">; sumi is i16, 64 lanes; y is a scalar int8</span>
<span class="op">vlm.v   </span>  v0, (plane c)   <span class="cm">; 64 row bits</span>
<span class="op">vsub.vx </span>  v8, v8, y       <span class="cm">; -y everywhere</span>
<span class="op">vadd.vx </span>  v8, v8, 2y, v0.t <span class="cm">; +2y where set</span>
<span class="cm">; net effect: +y or -y, per lane, per column</span></div>
        <dl class="tally">
          <dt>lanes busy</dt><dd class="hi">64 of 64 rows</dd>
          <dt>MACs per vector op</dt><dd class="hi">21.3</dd>
          <dt>vector ops per 64x128 tile</dt><dd>384</dd>
          <dt>horizontal reductions</dt><dd class="hi">0</dd>
        </dl>
        <p class="cap">
          LMUL follows VLEN so the 64 fp32 accumulators exactly fill the machine: i16 m1 / f32 m2
          at VLEN=1024, i16 m2 / f32 m4 at VLEN=512.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="cards c2">
      <div class="card">
        <p class="tagline">Constraint solved</p>
        <h3>No zvfh needed</h3>
        <p>
          The 64 fp16 row scales are expanded to fp32 with plain integer bit math, and the int16
          group sums are widened with <code>vsext</code> + <code>vfcvt</code> rather than
          <code>vfwcvt.f.x.v</code>, which some RTLs reject. The kernel runs on a
          <code>zve32f</code> machine.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <p class="tagline">Gate</p>
        <h3>Rows must be a multiple of 64</h3>
        <p>
          A tensor whose row count is not divisible by 64 falls back to the stage 01 kernel.
          In Bonsai-4B that is the tied LM head, <code>[2560 x 151669]</code> &mdash; 151669 mod 64 = 53
          &mdash; which is still roughly a quarter of an optimized decode pass.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ STAGE 03 ============ -->
<section class="sec" id="s03">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="stage-head">
      <div class="stage-num">03</div>
      <div class="stage-titles">
        <p class="stage-kicker">Software prefetch &mdash; zicbop</p>
        <h2>Now that the math is cheap, the kernel is waiting on memory</h2>
        <div class="stage-cyc">
          <span class="chip win">decode <b>1,389,236</b> &nbsp;1.54x over 02</span>
          <span class="chip">prefill 1,651,949 &nbsp;1.03x</span>
          <span class="chip">results unchanged</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="panels two">
      <div class="panel">
        <div class="panel-label"><span>Why the hardware cannot do it</span></div>
        <p class="cap" style="margin-top:0">
          The gemv streams one <code>block_q1_0x64</code> after another: <b>1152 bytes = 18 cache
          lines</b> each, touched once and never reused. The core's hardware prefetcher is shallow
          (<code>L3C_PFP_LINE_NUM=0</code>, 4 D-cache entries), so it cannot run far enough ahead
          to cover the L3 or DRAM latency.
        </p>
        <div class="ops" style="margin-top:1rem"><span class="cm">; top of block l, 18 lines of block l+1</span>
<span class="op">for</span> o <span class="op">in</span> 0, 64, ..., 1088:
    <span class="hot">prefetch.r</span>  (&amp;bx[l+1] + o)</div>
        <p class="cap">Pure hint. The numbers coming out of the kernel do not change at all.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="panel">
        <div class="panel-label"><span>Distance sweep</span><span class="r">decode, K=51</span></div>
        <div class="tablewrap" style="margin-top:0;border:0">
          <table style="min-width:0">
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>DIST=0 (off)</td><td>2,144,213</td><td>1.00x</td></tr>
              <tr><td>DIST=1</td><td class="win">1,391,650</td><td class="win">1.54x</td></tr>
              <tr><td>DIST=2</td><td>worse</td><td></td></tr>
              <tr><td>DIST=4</td><td>worse</td><td></td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="cap">
          One block ahead already hides the latency; looking further only adds cache and MSHR
          pressure. <b>Prefill gains almost nothing (1.03x)</b> because its M-tile of 4 rows
          already reuses each weight block four times, which hid the stall by itself.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ STAGE 04 ============ -->
<section class="sec" id="s04">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="stage-head">
      <div class="stage-num">04</div>
      <div class="stage-titles">
        <p class="stage-kicker">Andes V5 XAndesVDot &mdash; nds.vd4dots.vv</p>
        <h2>One instruction, 256 multiply-accumulates</h2>
        <div class="stage-cyc">
          <span class="chip win">decode <b>457,092</b> &nbsp;3.04x over 03</span>
          <span class="chip win">prefill <b>931,754</b> &nbsp;1.77x</span>
          <span class="chip">bit-identical to 03</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1.6rem">
      <p>
        <code>nds.vd4dots.vv</code> is a <strong>stock Andes vendor instruction</strong>, not a
        custom ACE extension. At <code>e32</code> it computes, for every 32-bit lane:
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="ops" style="max-width:62ch;margin-top:.4rem"><span class="hot">vd[j] += vs1[4j+0]*vs2[4j+0] + vs1[4j+1]*vs2[4j+1]
        + vs1[4j+2]*vs2[4j+2] + vs1[4j+3]*vs2[4j+3]</span>
<span class="cm">; int8 x int8 accumulated into the i32 lane
; vd, vs1, vs2 all at the same LMUL</span></div>

    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <p>
        At VLEN=1024, <code>e32/m2</code> is exactly <strong>64 i32 lanes = 64 rows</strong>, and
        that same register group seen as bytes is <strong>256 bytes = 64 rows x 4 columns</strong>.
        The instruction's operand shape and the repacked tile are the same object. The only work
        left was to make the bits arrive in that order.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="panels">
      <div class="panel">
        <div class="panel-label"><span>The operand shape</span><span class="r">8 of 64 lanes shown</span></div>
        <div class="scrollx">
          <div class="regrow">
            <span class="regname"><b>v12</b> weights, i8 view</span>
            <span class="lanes" data-reg="w" data-lanes="8" data-seed="23"></span>
            <span class="ellip">... 64 lanes</span>
          </div>
          <div class="regrow">
            <span class="regname"><b>v16</b> activations, i8 view</span>
            <span class="lanes" data-reg="a" data-lanes="8" data-seed="5"></span>
            <span class="ellip">same 4 quants in every lane</span>
          </div>
          <div class="collapse" data-collapse="8"></div>
          <div class="regrow">
            <span class="regname"><b>v8</b> accumulator, i32 view</span>
            <span class="lanes" data-reg="acc" data-lanes="8"></span>
            <span class="ellip">one row per lane</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="cap">
          Lane <em>j</em> is output row <em>j</em>. Its four bytes are that row's four consecutive
          K columns. One instruction retires <b>64 rows x 4 columns = 256 MACs</b>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="panels wide-left">
      <div class="panel">
        <div class="panel-label"><span>Layout, permuted again</span><span class="r">same 1152 B</span></div>
        <div class="bytemap">
          <div class="bseg d" style="flex:128"><span>d[64]</span><span class="n">128 B</span></div>
          <div class="bseg q hi" style="flex:256"><span>plane 0 &mdash; columns 0..3</span><span class="n">32 B = 256 bits</span></div>
          <div class="bseg q striped" style="flex:768"><span>planes 1 .. 31</span><span class="n">32 B each</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="bytemap-key"><span>byte 0</span><span>byte 1151</span></div>
        <p class="cap">
          <b>Bit index <code>(c/4)*256 + 4*j + (c%4)</code>.</b> A plane now covers four columns.
          Bit <code>4j + (c%4)</code> is byte lane <code>4j + (c%4)</code> of the register group
          &mdash; the position the dot reads for row <em>j</em>, column <em>c</em>. Same bytes as
          stage 02, only permuted.
        </p>
        <div class="scrollx" style="margin-top:1rem">
          <div class="regrow">
            <span class="regname"><b>one vlm</b> 32 B mask</span>
            <span class="lanes" data-reg="nib" data-lanes="8" data-seed="23"></span>
            <span class="ellip">... 64 nibbles</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="cap">
          Read as 64 nibbles: nibble <em>j</em> is row <em>j</em>, and its four bits are columns
          c..c+3. A single <code>vmerge</code> turns the whole mask into 256 bytes of +1 / -1.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="panel">
        <div class="panel-label"><span>Inner loop</span><span class="r">per 4 columns</span></div>
        <div class="ops"><span class="op">vlm.v         </span> v0, (plane c/4) <span class="cm">; 32 B</span>
<span class="op">vmerge.vxm    </span> v12, vneg1, 1, v0
<span class="op">vmv.v.x       </span> v16, a          <span class="cm">; 4 quants</span>
<span class="hot">nds.vd4dots.vv</span> v8, v12, v16   <span class="cm">; 256 MACs</span></div>
        <dl class="tally">
          <dt>lanes busy</dt><dd class="hi">64 rows x 4 cols</dd>
          <dt>MACs per vector op</dt><dd class="hi">64.0</dd>
          <dt>vector ops per 64x128 tile</dt><dd class="hi">128</dd>
          <dt>mask loads per block</dt><dd class="hi">32 x 32 B</dd>
          <dt>&nbsp;&nbsp;was, stage 02</dt><dd>128 x 8 B</dd>
        </dl>
        <p class="cap">
          Same bytes, <b>a quarter of the load operations</b>. That is why the in-model win (3.04x)
          beats the standalone cache-resident lab (2.52x) &mdash; the lab could not see the load
          traffic.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="cards c3">
      <div class="card">
        <p class="tagline">One authority</p>
        <h3>The layout cannot drift</h3>
        <p>
          Both bit orders live in one function, <code>q1_0x64_sign_bit()</code>. The repack writer
          and every reader call it. A numeric test can never catch a layout mistake here &mdash;
          writer and reader share the bug, so it cancels. Only a codegen gate can.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <p class="tagline">gemv activation side</p>
        <h3>Restage, do not gather</h3>
        <p>
          The dot wants 4 activation quants as one 32-bit element, but <code>block_q8_0</code> puts
          <code>qs</code> at offset 2, so every other group is 2 mod 4 and the core faults on a
          misaligned 32-bit vector element load. Copy the quants once per call through byte
          load/store (EEW=8 has no alignment rule). Under 1%.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <p class="tagline">gemm activation side</p>
        <h3>De-interleave with vnsrl</h3>
        <p>
          The x4 activation block stores <code>qs[c*4 + m]</code>, but row <em>m</em> needs its four
          columns contiguous &mdash; a stride-4 gather. Instead peel the byte lanes apart once per
          row tile with <code>vnsrl</code> (u32 to 2x u16 to 4x u8): 6 shifts and 4 stores per 32
          columns, reused by every column tile.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ GEMV vs GEMM ============ -->
<section class="sec" id="gemvgemm">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">The two tiles</p>
      <h2>Same instruction, mirrored</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Decode multiplies by one activation vector; prefill by four at once. The dot is used the
          same way in both, but which operand is reused flips &mdash; and that decides how the
          registers are budgeted.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="panels two">
      <div class="panel">
        <div class="panel-label"><span>gemv / decode</span><span class="r">q1_vd4dots_x4_m2</span></div>
        <div class="scrollx">
          <div class="regrow">
            <span class="regname">weights</span>
            <span class="lanes" data-reg="tile" data-tiles="4" data-labels="w0,w1,w2,w3"></span>
          </div>
          <div class="regrow">
            <span class="regname">activations</span>
            <span class="lanes" data-reg="tile" data-tiles="4" data-labels="a0,a1,a2,a3" data-kind="a"></span>
          </div>
          <div class="regrow">
            <span class="regname">accumulator</span>
            <span class="lanes" data-reg="tile" data-tiles="1" data-labels="acc" data-kind="acc" data-w="4"></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="cap">
          <b>Four different columns, one accumulator.</b> Four dots are chained into the same
          register so the pipeline stays fed while each result is still in flight.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="panel">
        <div class="panel-label"><span>gemm / prefill</span><span class="r">q1_vd4dots_4acc_m2</span></div>
        <div class="scrollx">
          <div class="regrow">
            <span class="regname">weights</span>
            <span class="lanes" data-reg="tile" data-tiles="1" data-labels="w" data-w="4"></span>
          </div>
          <div class="regrow">
            <span class="regname">activations</span>
            <span class="lanes" data-reg="tile" data-tiles="4" data-labels="a0,a1,a2,a3" data-kind="a"></span>
          </div>
          <div class="regrow">
            <span class="regname">accumulators</span>
            <span class="lanes" data-reg="tile" data-tiles="4" data-labels="acc0,acc1,acc2,acc3" data-kind="acc"></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="cap">
          <b>One weight vector, four output rows.</b> The weight stream is read once and used four
          times, which is why prefill was already efficient before stage 03 and gained little
          from prefetching.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="cards c2">
      <div class="card">
        <p class="tagline">Register budget</p>
        <h3>24 of 32 vector registers</h3>
        <p>
          At <code>e32/m2</code> the gemm holds 4 accumulators, 4 float sums, the row scales, the
          weight vector, the activation scratch and the constant -1 vector. There is no room left
          for a per-row float accumulator, so the activation scale is folded into the row scale
          instead. Same sum, one extra <code>vfmul</code> per group:
        </p>
        <div class="ops" style="margin-top:.6rem;font-size:.68rem">sum_l dx * (sum_k da[k]*acc[k])
  == sum_l sum_k (dx*da[k]) * acc[k]</div>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <p class="tagline">Where the cost went</p>
        <h3>Prefill did not gain less because it is worse</h3>
        <p>
          Cycles per multiply-add on the same node, decode versus prefill: at stage 03 it was
          0.2650 versus 0.1576, so prefill was already 1.68x more efficient. At stage 04 both land
          at 0.0872 and 0.0889 &mdash; the same. Prefill's smaller headline ratio is a better
          starting point, not a weaker kernel.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ STAGE 05 ============ -->
<section class="sec" id="s05">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="stage-head">
      <div class="stage-num">05</div>
      <div class="stage-titles">
        <p class="stage-kicker">Codegen &mdash; the vtype churn</p>
        <h2>Forty percent of the loop was changing element width</h2>
        <div class="stage-cyc">
          <span class="chip win">decode <b>362,799</b> &nbsp;1.26x over 04</span>
          <span class="chip win">prefill <b>818,314</b> &nbsp;1.14x</span>
          <span class="chip">no source-level change to the math</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1.6rem">
      <p>
        The kernel keeps switching between two views of the same registers: the <strong>byte
        view</strong> for the mask load and sign expand, and the <strong>32-bit view</strong> for
        the broadcast and the dot. Every switch needs a <code>vsetvli</code>, which does no
        arithmetic. GCC makes it worse: it cannot see inside an inline asm block, so it assumes
        each one left <code>vtype</code> unknown and re-issues its own on top.
      </p>
      <p>
        Result before the fix: <strong>four <code>vsetvli</code> per dot</strong> in the gemv.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="panels two">
      <div class="panel">
        <div class="panel-label"><span>Before</span><span class="r">4.00 vsetvli per dot</span></div>
        <div class="ops"><span class="bad">vsetvli</span> e8       <span class="cm">; byte view</span>
<span class="op">vlm.v  </span> v0
<span class="op">vmerge </span> v12
<span class="bad">vsetvli</span> e32      <span class="cm">; 32-bit view</span>
<span class="op">vmv.v.x</span> v16
<span class="hot">nds.vd4dots.vv</span> v8, v12, v16
<span class="bad">vsetvli</span> e32      <span class="cm">; GCC re-issues</span>
<span class="bad">vsetvli</span> e8       <span class="cm">; next column</span>
<span class="cm">; ... repeat, once per single dot</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="panel">
        <div class="panel-label"><span>After</span><span class="r">0.94 vsetvli per dot</span></div>
        <div class="ops"><span class="op">vsetvli</span> e8       <span class="cm">; once for 4 columns</span>
<span class="op">vlm.v  </span> v0 x4
<span class="op">vmerge </span> v12 x4
<span class="cm">; one asm block, everything e32 inside:</span>
<span class="hot">vsetvli</span> e32, m2
<span class="op">vmv.v.x</span> s0 ; <span class="op">vmv.v.x</span> s1
<span class="hot">nds.vd4dots.vv</span> acc, w0, s0
<span class="hot">nds.vd4dots.vv</span> acc, w1, s1
<span class="op">vmv.v.x</span> s0 ; <span class="op">vmv.v.x</span> s1
<span class="hot">nds.vd4dots.vv</span> acc, w2, s0
<span class="hot">nds.vd4dots.vv</span> acc, w3, s1</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table>
        <caption>Static count, -O3, between the first and last dot</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Kernel</th><th>vsetvli / dot, before</th><th>after</th><th>instrs / dot, before</th><th>after</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>gemv</td><td>4.00</td><td class="win">0.94</td><td>10.00</td><td class="win">7.90</td></tr>
          <tr><td>gemm</td><td>2.00</td><td class="win">0.52</td><td>9.00</td><td class="win">4.13</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="cards c2">
      <div class="card trap">
        <p class="tagline">Did not work</p>
        <h3>Grouping the two widths in the source</h3>
        <p>
          Writing all four mask loads first and all four dots after only got the gemv from 4.00 to
          2.00. The scheduler interleaves them again. The fix has to be inside a single asm block,
          where the scheduler cannot reach.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card trap">
        <p class="tagline">Did not work</p>
        <h3>Four scratch registers instead of two</h3>
        <p>
          The idea was to double the distance between each broadcast and the dot that consumes it.
          No spills, 4.06 instructions per dot instead of 4.13 &mdash; and <b>4.5% slower</b>
          (855,476 versus 818,314). Producer-consumer distance is not what limits the gemm.
          Reverted.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="pull">
      <p>
        After this stage the two paths are bound by different things. Cycles per issued
        instruction: the gemv went 2.23 to 2.24 &mdash; <strong>unchanged</strong>, so it is purely
        instruction-issue bound and every instruction removed became a cycle saved, one for one.
        The gemm went 2.53 to <strong>4.84</strong>. It is no longer issue bound: roughly 11 cycles
        per dot are stall, and cutting more instructions will not help prefill.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ TRAPS ============ -->
<section class="sec" id="traps">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Also shipped</p>
      <h2>Three traps that cost the whole win, silently</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Each of these produced <em>correct results</em> while deleting the optimization, so no
          numeric test could see it. Each one now has a comment in the source and a script that
          fails the build.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="cards c3">
      <div class="card trap">
        <p class="tagline">Trap 01 &mdash; 18x</p>
        <h3>The stride-0 broadcast</h3>
        <p>
          Handed "load a word, then splat it", GCC contracts the pair into
          <code>vlse32.v vd,(rs1),zero</code>. A stride-0 strided load is <b>element-serial</b> on
          this core. Since stage 05 the broadcast is written by hand as <code>vmv.v.x</code> inside
          the asm block, taking the quads as plain GPRs, so the contraction is impossible.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card trap">
        <p class="tagline">Trap 02 &mdash; fault</p>
        <h3>Misaligned vector element load</h3>
        <p>
          The core faults on a misaligned 32-bit vector element load. <code>block_q8_0</code> puts
          <code>qs</code> at offset 2 and is 34 bytes, so every other group is 2 mod 4. Restaging
          through byte load/store fixes it; <code>block_q8_0x4</code> is 4-byte aligned and needs
          no restaging.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card trap">
        <p class="tagline">Trap 03 &mdash; silent DCE</p>
        <h3>always_inline is load-bearing</h3>
        <p>
          A prefetch has no observable effect, so whenever the compiler leaves the helper out of
          line, <code>ipa-pure-const</code> marks it pure and dead-code elimination deletes every
          call. No warning, results still correct. GCC 14.2 does exactly this at <code>-Os</code>,
          which had removed the entire stage 03 win from the MinSizeRel build.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="panels two">
      <div class="panel">
        <div class="panel-label"><span>check-vd4dots.sh</span><span class="r">at -O2, -O3, -Os</span></div>
        <div class="ops"><span class="cm">; per KERNEL, not per entry point -</span>
<span class="cm">; the d4 kernels stay out of line</span>
nds.vd4dots  &gt; 0   <span class="cm">; else it fell back, 3x slow</span>
prefetch.r   &gt; 0   <span class="cm">; trap 03</span>
vsetvli/dot &lt;= 1   <span class="cm">; stage 05 budget</span>
vlse*       == 0   <span class="cm">; trap 01</span></div>
        <p class="cap">
          It rewrites the build's own compile line out of <code>compile_commands.json</code>, so
          the gate tests the flags the build actually uses.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="panel">
        <div class="panel-label"><span>test-repack-q1_0</span><span class="r">13 cases x 2 layouts</span></div>
        <p class="cap" style="margin-top:0">
          Two of the thirteen exist only because they are the only shapes where the gemm's row-tile
          loop advances more than once, so they are the only ones that check the activation and
          output strides. Proved by fault injection: swapping two rows in the de-interleave fails
          exactly those two and nothing else.
        </p>
        <p class="cap">
          Stages 03, 04 and 05 are <b>bit-identical</b> to stage 02 &mdash; every error figure is
          unchanged, not merely within tolerance.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ RESULTS ============ -->
<section class="sec" id="results">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Measured</p>
      <h2>Every Q1_0 node in one layer</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Layer 7 of Bonsai-4B contains all six distinct Q1_0 shapes in the model, across a 9.5x
          range of node size. The win is <strong>shape-invariant</strong>: 3.03x to 3.16x from the
          dot on every one of them.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table>
        <caption>Decode / gemv, nrows=2, cycles</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Node</th><th>Shape</th>
            <th>01 baseline</th><th>03 prefetch</th><th>04 vd4dots</th>
            <th>04 / 03</th><th>04 / 01</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>attn_q</td><td>2560x4096</td><td>17,973,840</td><td>5,487,181</td><td>1,744,082</td><td class="win">3.15x</td><td class="win">10.31x</td></tr>
          <tr><td>attn_v</td><td>2560x1024</td><td>4,476,673</td><td>1,389,236</td><td>457,092</td><td class="win">3.04x</td><td class="win">9.79x</td></tr>
          <tr><td>attn_k</td><td>2560x1024</td><td>4,512,573</td><td>1,391,142</td><td>457,553</td><td class="win">3.04x</td><td class="win">9.86x</td></tr>
          <tr><td>attn_output</td><td>4096x2560</td><td>18,316,032</td><td>5,492,936</td><td>1,777,405</td><td class="win">3.09x</td><td class="win">10.31x</td></tr>
          <tr><td>ffn_gate</td><td>2560x9728</td><td>42,178,724</td><td>12,997,485</td><td>4,118,497</td><td class="win">3.16x</td><td class="win">10.24x</td></tr>
          <tr><td>ffn_up</td><td>2560x9728</td><td>42,134,940</td><td>12,997,000</td><td>4,118,579</td><td class="win">3.16x</td><td class="win">10.23x</td></tr>
          <tr><td>ffn_down</td><td>9728x2560</td><td>49,054,708</td><td>12,997,483</td><td>4,284,663</td><td class="win">3.03x</td><td class="win">11.45x</td></tr>
          <tr class="total"><td>Layer 7 total</td><td></td><td>178,647,490</td><td>52,752,463</td><td>16,957,871</td><td class="win">3.11x</td><td class="win">10.54x</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="cap" style="max-width:74ch">
      This sweep's stage 03 versus stage 01 per-layer figure is 3.386x, which reproduces the 3.39x
      an independent earlier sweep measured &mdash; the two sessions agree. Stage 05 was measured
      separately on two nodes: attn_v decode 362,799 and prefill 818,314, ffn_down prefill
      5,683,029 (1.17x).
    </p>

    <div class="cards c2">
      <div class="card">
        <p class="tagline">Outlier, both ways</p>
        <h3>ffn_down</h3>
        <p>
          Longest reduction dimension (K=9728), so prefetch helps it most and the dot adds the
          least on top &mdash; and it still ends up the best combined result in the sweep at
          11.45x. At 3.5 MB it does not fit the 2 MB L3, so weight reuse still buys real memory
          traffic there: its <b>gemm is 1.29x better per MAC than its gemv</b>, which no other node
          shows.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <p class="tagline">Correction on the record</p>
        <h3>The "instruction-issue floor" was not one</h3>
        <p>
          At stage 04 the fastest configuration measured was 328,865 cycles, with weights in local
          memory, and it was written down as an instruction-issue floor. It was not a floor &mdash;
          it was an instruction count. Stage 05 beat it at <b>362,799 with no local memory at
          all</b>, and stage 05 plus local memory was never measured.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ WHAT IS LEFT ============ -->
<section class="sec" id="left">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Open</p>
      <h2>What is left</h2>
    </div>

    <ul class="kv">
      <li>
        <span class="k">Prefill's stall</span>
        <span class="v">About 11 cycles per dot are stall, cause not established. Two build targets
        already exist that would settle it: prefetch off, and weights in local memory, both on the
        prefill node. If neither moves the number, the limit is the dot's own throughput at
        <code>e32/m2</code> and prefill is done until the ISA changes.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="k">Local memory</span>
        <span class="v">Worth 1.39x at stage 04 and never re-measured since. The caveat cuts the
        other way for big models: attn_v's 360 KB working set fits the 2 MB L3, but a 27B ffn_down
        is 12 MB per tensor, where L3 is useless and local memory should be worth more.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="k">A 1-bit weight operand</span>
        <span class="v">The remaining pot is compute, not memory. An instruction taking the sign
        mask directly would drop the <code>vmerge</code> and cut weight register traffic by 8x.
        It attacks a different bottleneck than local memory, so the two stack.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="k">VLEN=512</span>
        <span class="v">Needs the <code>e32/m4</code> shape of the same kernel. Blocked on a
        hybrid-mode halt bug on that engine, not on the kernel.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="k">The LM head</span>
        <span class="v">Bonsai-4B ties its output projection to the token embedding,
        <code>[2560 x 151669]</code>. 151669 mod 64 = 53, so it fails the repack gate and still runs
        the stage 01 kernel &mdash; roughly a quarter of an otherwise optimized decode pass.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="k">Unexplored vendor ops</span>
        <span class="v"><code>nds.vqmacc.*</code>, the sub-int loads <code>nds.vln8.v</code> and
        <code>nds.vle4.v</code>, <code>nds.vfpmadb/t.vf</code>, <code>nds.vfwcvt.f.b.v</code> are all
        in this core's ISA string and none have been tried.</span>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

